.TH EUCLID-CLI-EAP-SET-APPLICATION-LIMITS 1 "November 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-eap-set-application-limits \- set how much memory and CPU each of an application's instances may use
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli eap set-application-limits
.BR \-\-application\-id | \-n
.I NAME
[\fB\-\-memory\-limit\fR|\fB\-m\fR \fISIZE\fR]
[\fB\-\-cpu\-limit\fR|\fB\-c\fR \fICORES\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Sets the most memory and the most CPU one instance of an application may
use, without restarting anything. A limit not named is left as it is.
.PP
The limit is per instance, not per application: an application may use up
to its maximum instance count times this, which is a number that can be
worked out in advance. A pool-wide budget shared between instances would
shrink each one as the pool grew, which is the opposite of what scaling is
for.
.PP
The worker that starts an instance makes the limit its cgroup's
.IR memory.max ,
sets
.I memory.high
a little below it (see
.I euclid.worker.memory\-high\-percent
in
.BR euclid-wrk (8))
so that an instance approaching its ceiling is slowed down and reclaimed
before it is killed, and gives it no swap. An instance that goes over the
limit anyway is killed by the kernel \- that instance, not whichever process
the kernel would otherwise have picked on the whole host \- and is reported
as
.B OOM_KILLED
rather than
.BR CRASHED ,
in
.B emm list-modules
and in the application's state. After three of those in a row the worker
stops restarting the instance, because a fourth start under the same limit
will end the same way; it tries again once the limit or the application
changes.
.SS CPU
The CPU limit becomes the instance cgroup's
.IR cpu.max :
a quota per 100 ms period, so 1.5 cores is 150 ms of CPU in every 100 ms.
Over it an instance is throttled until the next period \- slowed, never
killed. A throttled instance reports higher utilisation and the
autoscaler adds instances, which is the right answer to an application
that needs more CPU than one instance is allowed. How often it happens is
in
.IR application\-cpu\-throttled\-percent ,
the share of periods in which an instance ran out of its quota.
.SS Admission
A memory limit is checked against what the nodes offer before it is
stored. Refused with HTTP 409 when one instance would be larger than any
node offers applications, or when the application's minimum instance
count times the limit, on top of what every other running application in
the account has committed the same way, is more than the nodes offer. The
same check is made by
.BR create-application ,
.BR update-application ,
.B scale-application
(when the floor rises) and
.BR start-application .
.PP
What a node offers is its memory less what it keeps back for everything
else on the host \- see
.I euclid.worker.memory\-reserve\-mb
in
.BR euclid-wrk (8);
the local node keeps half the host unless
.I euclid.modules.eap.local\-node.memory\-reserve\-mb
says otherwise.
.I euclid.modules.eap.memory\-overcommit\-ratio
(default 1.0) lets the limits add up to more than that, deliberately: a
limit is a ceiling, not a reservation. Applications without a memory
limit commit nothing and are never refused.
.PP
The same rule holds at scale-up: an instance the autoscaler wants is
placed only on a node with room left for its limit. When none has, the
pool stops growing and the manager logs
.I "Not adding an instance: no node has ... MiB left for it"
with each node's committed and offered memory, rather than starting an
instance to be killed.
.SS When it takes effect
Instances started from now on \- a new one, a replacement, a restart \- get
the new limit. Running instances keep the one they started under until
.BR euclid-cli-eap-restart-application (1).
That is deliberate: a JVM sizes its heap once, from the limit it starts
under, and lowering the ceiling under one that planned for more is an OOM
kill waiting for its next busy minute.
.PP
.BR euclid-cli-eap-update-application (1)
with
.B \-\-memory\-limit
sets the same field and restarts the application, like any other change to
its definition.
.SS Where it is enforced
On a node whose
.I euclid\-wrk.service
carries
.IR "Delegate=memory cpu pids" ,
as the shipped unit does. Anywhere else \- a worker started by hand, cgroup
v1, Windows, macOS \- the instance runs without a limit and the worker logs a
warning saying so each time it starts one. What a node actually enforces is
in its
.I application\-memory\-limit\-mb
series.
.SS The JVM heap
A JVM started under a limit sizes its heap from it, but by default takes
only a quarter: under a 2 GiB limit it gives itself 512 MiB of heap and can
run out of heap with most of its allowance unused. So a JAVA, JAVA21 or
JAVA25 instance whose limit is enforced is started with
.B \-XX:MaxRAMPercentage=75
(see
.I euclid.worker.java\-max\-ram\-percent
in
.BR euclid-wrk (8)),
and the worker logs that it did. It is not added where no limit is
enforced \- an unlimited JVM would take that share of the whole host \- nor
for an application with a command of its own.
.PP
An application that sizes its heap itself keeps its own setting: an
.BR \-Xmx ,
.BR \-XX:MaxHeapSize ,
.BR \-XX:MaxRAMPercentage ,
.B \-XX:MaxRAM
or
.B \-XX:MaxRAMFraction
in
.IR JAVA_TOOL_OPTIONS ,
.I JDK_JAVA_OPTIONS
or
.I _JAVA_OPTIONS
in its environment. Not in its arguments: those follow the jar and are
the application's own, so the JVM never sees them.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-application\-id ", " \-n " " \fINAME\fR
Name of the application, in the namespace the session works in.
.TP
.BR \-\-memory\-limit ", " \-m " " \fISIZE\fR
A number of MiB, or a number with
.B M
or
.B G
after it \- 512M, 2G, 1.5G. 0 removes the limit.
.TP
.BR \-\-cpu\-limit ", " \-c " " \fICORES\fR
In cores: 1.5, 0.25, 4. 0 removes the limit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Non-zero if the application does not exist, if neither limit is given, if
the size cannot be read, if a memory limit is below 64 MiB or a CPU limit
below 0.01 cores and not 0, or if admission refuses the memory limit.
.SH EXAMPLES
Hold each instance of an importer to 2 GiB:
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli eap set-application-limits --application-id parsing --memory-limit 2G
euclid-cli eap restart-application --application-id parsing
.fi
.RE
.PP
Hold each instance to one and a half cores:
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli eap set-application-limits --application-id transformation --cpu-limit 1.5
.fi
.RE
.PP
Remove the memory limit again:
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli eap set-application-limits --application-id parsing --memory-limit 0
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-eap (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eap-update-application (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eap-restart-application (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eap-scale-application (1),
.BR euclid-wrk (8)
